window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"06-06","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"06-06-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-01/","title_he":"א – דינא דמלכותא","title_en":"Dina De-malkhuta","body_en":"<p>One of the basic rules in the laws of money and judgement is 'dina de-malkhuta dina', that is, the law of the kingdom is law, and a person is obligated to keep and fulfil the laws of the kingdom, whether laws connected to income tax and customs, or laws concerning public order, such as traffic laws and the like.</p>\n<p>Several explanations were given for this. The first is that the residents of the state accept the kingdom upon themselves and forgo to it part of their rights, and agree that the kingdom will set laws and manage the order of public life. Indeed, many citizens always complain about the level of tax or about certain laws, but in any case, as long as they do not openly rebel and overthrow the government, they acknowledge and agree that the present situation of the rule of the kingdom is preferable for them to the chaos that might prevail without it, and thereby they grant the kingdom the right to set laws and collect taxes.</p>\n<p>Another explanation was mentioned: that in essence the land of the whole state belongs to the kingdom by right of conquest, for the kingdom, through its military and economic power, guards the state; it follows, then, that all the lands of the state belong to the kingdom, and therefore whoever lives in that state must obey the owner of the house, namely the kingdom. And although there are private owners for every courtyard and house, in any case their ownership rests on the ownership of the kingdom as a whole. For had there not been that kingdom, who knows whether that courtyard and that house would have remained in their hands.</p>\n<p>An elected government has at least the authority that a kingdom has. For regarding a government, the public consent to its rule is much broader, since the public chose the government directly and thereby accepted upon itself the decisions that that government will make. And even the minority that is not represented in the government agreed to accept upon itself the decision of the majority. And if a person comes and says, \"I do not agree with the democratic method\", in any case the power of the government is certainly not less than the power of a king who ruled his people by the power of his soldiers' swords, about whom too they said 'dina de-malkhuta dina'. If so, in summary, there is a prohibition to evade tax or to smuggle past customs, and whoever transgresses and evades his taxes transgresses the Torah prohibition \"You shall not rob\". And so it is ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (ḤM 369:6).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-02/","title_he":"ב – גדרי דינא דמלכותא","title_en":"The Limits of Dina De-malkhuta","body_en":"<p>Regarding the rule 'dina de-malkhuta dina', the sages of the Rishonim noted precisely that it is specifically the law of the kingdom that is law, but not the law of the king. That is, if a king arises and decrees decrees that contradict reason and accepted morality, these decrees have no validity. Only regulations and laws generally accepted in the other kingdoms have validity.</p>\n<p>As one of the Rishonim, the author of the 'Nimukei Yosef', wrote in his commentary on tractate Bava Batra (54b): \"And all the authorities agreed that from the fact that they said 'dina de-malkhuta', it is implied that they said it only about matters that are the laws of kings, for all kings have known laws; but what an oppressive official takes by force is not law.\" The Ramban too (there) wrote that they said 'dina de-malkhuta dina' only regarding laws known in all kingdoms, which all kings practise. And so too wrote the Ritva (on the Rif, Nedarim 28a): what one governor does for the moment, whether to an individual or to many, or when he decrees a new decree that was not practised in the days of the fathers, this is not law but violence and robbery.</p>\n<p>From all this we learn that the kingdom or the government may not set laws that are not accepted in all kingdoms, and this includes that the laws must be laws that do not discriminate against or deprive a certain person or group. And there have already been cases in the history of Israel in which special laws were legislated discriminating against the Jews for the bad, and the great ones of Israel ruled that these laws do not have the validity of 'dina de-malkhuta'. The reason for this is that the people accepted upon itself the kingdom or the government on condition that it rule fairly, as is accepted and customary in the world, and it has no authority to deviate from this; and if it deviated, its law is not law (see Tzitz Eliezer 16:49).</p>\n<p>However, all this applies specifically to a clear injustice, whose unfairness pierces to the foundation of the kingdom or the government. But ordinary corruption and discrimination, which are found in almost every government, do not annul the rule 'dina de-malkhuta dina'. For if every corruption were to annul this rule, there would be no place in the world in which the rule 'dina de-malkhuta dina' would apply.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-03/","title_he":"ג – דינא דמלכותא בארץ ישראל","title_en":"Dina De-malkhuta in the Land of Israel","body_en":"<p>There is a central question: about which kingdom was the rule 'dina de-malkhuta dina' said? Was it about every kingdom, whether a kingdom abroad or in the Land, whether of Jews or of gentiles, or was the rule that their laws are law said only regarding kingdoms abroad?</p>\n<p>According to the Ran and the Rashba (in their commentaries on Nedarim 28a), this rule was said only regarding a kingdom of gentiles outside the Land. For the foundation of the kingdom's power to legislate laws is based on the fact that the land belongs to the king or the kingdom by right of conquest, since only by the power of the kingdom's army are the borders of the state guarded. Therefore a person who wishes to live in that kingdom must accept its laws upon himself, and if he is not willing to accept its laws, he must look for another place. And there is no difference in this between a king and a government; therefore a Jew too who lives in democratic countries is obligated to keep the laws of the state, because 'dina de-malkhuta dina'. But all this can be said only regarding outside the Land, where the land can indeed belong to the kingdom; but the Land of Israel the Holy One, blessed be He, gave to the people of Israel, and no kingdom has permission to expel a Jew from it. It follows, then, that the kingdom has no ownership of the land, and therefore as long as the Jew has not accepted that kingdom upon himself, its laws have no validity regarding him.</p>\n<p>However, most of the Rishonim, among them the Rambam, disagree with their explanation and hold that there is no difference between the Land of Israel and outside the Land, and wherever there is a kingdom or a government, its laws and statutes obligate. For the validity of the rule 'dina de-malkhuta dina' does not depend on ownership of the land, but rests on the public consent to the kingdom: by the very fact that the public does not rebel and overthrow the government, it agrees to accept upon itself all the laws it legislates. If so, there is no difference between the Land of Israel and outside the Land, and in the Land of Israel too the laws of the kingdom or the government obligate, as long as they do not clash explicitly with the laws of the Torah. (The Rashba too, in his responsa vol. 2, 134, ruled so, not as he wrote in his commentary on Nedarim.)</p>\n<p>And so it was ruled in halakha according to the view of most of the poskim, that in the Land of Israel too the rule of 'dina de-malkhuta dina' applies; and so it is ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (ḤM 369:6), and so ruled the Aḥaronim of our time.</p>\n<p>And according to the 'Ḥatam Sofer' (ḤM 44), even the Ran and the Rashba would agree that the taxes accepted today must be paid in the Land of Israel too. For all that they said, that one is not obligated to pay taxes to the king in the Land, is because we do not live in the Land with his permission but with the permission of the Creator, and therefore we need not pay him taxes. But all this was said regarding taxes that the king takes for his private pleasure and for the upkeep of his palace and his court and the honour of his kingship. But when the imposition of taxes is intended for the sake of the citizens of the state, for example the needs of security, education, roads and the like, certainly everyone is obligated to pay. And one who does not pay his taxes not only transgresses the law of 'dina de-malkhuta dina', but also robs the share of the public (see Tzitz Eliezer 16:49; Yeḥaveh Da'at 5:64).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-04/","title_he":"ד – אין בכוח 'דינא דמלכותא' לבטל מצווה","title_en":"Dina De-malkhuta Has No Power to Annul a Mitzva","body_en":"<p>The most significant limitation that stands before the law of the kingdom is that any law or statute that contradicts the laws of the Torah has no binding validity. For it was said to Yehoshua bin Nun that as the leader of Israel he has authority to issue instructions, and every man who rebels against his word will be punished and put to death; but in the continuation of the verse his authority is qualified by the expression \"only be strong and of good courage\", as it is said (Yehoshua 1:16-18): \"And they answered Yehoshua, saying: All that you have commanded us we will do, and wherever you send us we will go. According as we hearkened to Moshe in all things, so will we hearken to you; only may the Lord your God be with you, as He was with Moshe. Whoever rebels against your commandment and does not hearken to your words in all that you command him shall be put to death; <b>only be strong and of good courage</b>.\" That is, Yehoshua's regulations obligate on condition that he fulfils the verse (ibid. 7): \"<b>Only be strong and very courageous</b>, to observe to do according to all the Torah which Moshe My servant commanded you; turn not from it to the right hand or to the left, that you may prosper wherever you go.\" But if he issues instructions contrary to the Torah, his words are void and one should not obey him (Sanhedrin 49a). And so the Rambam ruled (Laws of Kings 3:9) that if the king decreed to annul a mitzva, one does not obey him, because the words of the Holy One, blessed be He, take precedence over the words of the king, for the king himself is a servant of the King of the kings of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He.</p>\n<p>And if this is said about a king like Yehoshua bin Nun, who was appointed by Moshe our teacher, the greatest of the prophets of Israel, and the Sanhedrin and the people of Israel accepted him upon themselves, all the more so no king or government can issue instructions contrary to the mitzvot of the Torah. For if any government whatsoever legislates a law that would require desecration of Shabbat, or eating forbidden foods such as pork, or commands to uproot Jews from their Land and thereby annul the mitzva of settling the Land, according to the Torah these laws will have no validity, and the obligation will rest on all Israel to oppose their execution.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-05/","title_he":"ה – סמכות 'טובי העיר' לכנסת ולממשלה","title_en":"The Authority of 'the Leaders of the Town' for the Knesset and the Government","body_en":"<p>Until now we have dealt with the halakhic rule 'dina de-malkhuta dina', which establishes that every tax or customs duty that the kingdom imposes on its residents has halakhic validity. Now we will add that the validity of the taxes in the State of Israel obligates Israel more than in the other states. For the government was elected by the majority of the Jewish public, and if so, in addition to the validity of a kingdom, the government of Israel also has the validity of a 'ḥever ir' (town council). 'Ḥever ir' is the name of the leaders who were elected by the people of the town or the community, and they were generally called 'tuvei ha-ir' (the leaders of the town) or 'tuvei ha-kahal' (the leaders of the community). And the Rosh wrote in his responsa (kelal 6, siman 19) that it is simple that whatever the leaders of the community agree to do, in all the regulations of the community that they enact for individuals and for the many, stands firm even without a formal act of acquisition, and all their words are as though written and handed over. And he further wrote (there, siman 27) that the people of the town may punish and fine whoever transgresses their regulations.</p>\n<p>Several explanations were given for this halakha. From the words of the Mabit in his responsa (3:228) it is implied that the halakhic validity of the community's regulations stems from custom: since the public is accustomed to manage its affairs by choosing leaders who enact regulations and impose taxes and fines, a private person cannot transgress their custom, and he is obligated to follow the regulations of the community. There is a further reasoning: in all public affairs the community has the law of partners, for if they do not organise together in all that concerns public matters, all of them may be harmed; and therefore, perforce, all the people of the town and all the people of the state become partners and guarantors for one another (Maharam Schick). And further, the public has a right of 'ḥazaka' (established usage), for so it has always been customary that the community intervenes a little in the affairs of the individual by imposing taxes and customs for the needs of the public. And as is known, in anything in which the public has an established usage, the individual cannot annul it. For example, when a path passes through an individual's courtyard and for a long time the public passed along it, an established usage was fixed regarding walking along it, and the owner of the courtyard cannot prevent the public from passing along the path in his courtyard. And likewise regarding taxes, there is an established usage that the public takes tax from every individual.</p>\n<p>In summary, it is clear in practice that there is a complete obligation to pay taxes according to the law, both because of 'dina de-malkhuta dina' and because of the regulations of the community (Tzitz Eliezer 16:50). And according to most poskim, since the obligation to pay taxes is considered a monetary stipulation, it is consequently a Torah obligation, and one who evades taxes transgresses the prohibition of robbery.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-06/","title_he":"ו – קונה ומוכר ותשלומי מס","title_en":"Buyer, Seller and Paying Tax","body_en":"<p>It is good for a person to see paying taxes as a pleasant obligation, for through his payments he becomes a partner in absorbing immigration, in fortifying security, and in the whole process of building the State of Israel. Indeed, one cannot ignore the many shortcomings found among the public's elected representatives; but in any case, since the public elected them, and they have the validity of a kingdom and of the leaders of the community, everyone is obligated to pay the taxes that the Knesset and the government set.</p>\n<p>The test in fulfilling the obligation to pay taxes is difficult, for many times a person's lust for money drives him out of his mind. For example, it happens that a person wants to buy a certain product, and when he asks its price, the seller answers him: without a receipt five hundred shekels, and with a receipt six hundred. And likewise regarding doing work, sometimes the electrician offers: without a receipt five hundred, and with a receipt six hundred. And the test is difficult, for it is not easy to give up a hundred shekels. But the halakha establishes that it is forbidden to steal the tax, and therefore a person is obligated to say that he wishes to buy with a receipt. Indeed, in special cases, when the tradesman is a relative or a close friend, it is permitted to pay him without a receipt, on condition that their connection is so close that he could have done the work for him for free, so that the payment for the work can be counted as a kind of gift of appreciation and not as wages liable to tax.</p>\n<p>Question: Is the buyer obligated to demand a receipt and thereby ensure that the seller pays tax on the sale, or is it the seller's personal obligation to pay tax and give a receipt, while the buyer is not obligated to demand a receipt and fill in this matter the role of a policeman or tax collector?</p>\n<p>Answer: The buyer is not obligated to demand a receipt, and by buying without receiving a receipt he does not transgress a prohibition, as he would transgress if he said explicitly to the seller that he wants to buy cheaply without a receipt. But it is fitting for the buyer to ask for a receipt, since it may be that by the buyer not demanding the receipt he assists to some extent in a transgression. Further, the mitzva \"You shall not stand by the blood of your fellow\" (Vayikra 19:16) includes also the obligation to save one's fellow from monetary loss, as the Rambam wrote (Sefer Ha-mitzvot 297). And the public as a whole is not worse than a single individual; therefore it is fitting to ask for a receipt when buying, and thereby to see to it that the tax is indeed paid. But this is not an obligation, for the buyer cannot know for certain whether the seller is stealing the tax, since perhaps he intends afterwards to report the sale himself, and perhaps for other reasons he is exempt from payment (see Responsa Aseh Lekha Rav 6:80; 7:63).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-07/","title_he":"ז – מעמד בתי המשפט החילוניים","title_en":"The Status of the Secular Courts","body_en":"<p>The Torah commanded (Shemot 21:1): \"And these are the judgements which you shall set before them\", and the Sages explained (Gittin 88b): \"before them\" means specifically before the judges of Israel, and not before the judges of gentiles. And even if in that particular matter the gentile judges happen to rule according to the Torah, it is forbidden to go to be judged before them, as it is said (Devarim 1:17): \"for the judgement is God's\", and the judges of Israel, ordained one from the mouth of another back to Moshe our teacher, rule halakha on a divine mission. Indeed, about three hundred years after the destruction of the Second Temple, ordination (semikha) ceased in Israel, for one ordains only in the Land of Israel (see above 1:4), and the status of the judges changed. For until then the ordained Sages of Israel had independent authority to judge the people, and once ordination ceased, the Sages of Israel no longer have independent authority. Nevertheless, it is forbidden to be judged except before them, because they are considered agents of the judges who were ordained in the past, and by the power of their agency they rule halakha in all those common cases in which, if they did not judge them, harm would be caused.</p>\n<p>The position of the decisive majority of the rabbis is that the secular courts in Israel are considered 'arkha'ot shel goyim' (gentile courts), and it is forbidden to be judged before them, since their legal system is not according to the Torah of Israel. And although 'dina de-malkhuta dina', that is, the accepted government is permitted to legislate laws and enact regulations, and even the rabbinical courts, when they come to rule halakha, must take these laws into account as binding; nevertheless, no kingdom or government has the power to annul wholesale the mitzva to be judged by Torah law before the judges of Israel. Therefore every dispute must be clarified before judges who judge according to the Torah. And if the defendant refuses to be judged before a valid beit din, the plaintiff should go to a beit din or a rabbi who deals with monetary law and receive from him permission to go to the 'arkha'ot' in order to save his money, and then he may sue him in the 'arkha'ot', since he went to the 'arkha'ot' with the permission of the judges of Israel (see Rambam there, and in Dinei Mamonot by Rav Bazri, vol. 1, gate 10, chapter 5).</p>\n<p>According to many of the rabbis, the severe words of the Rambam also apply to the secular courts in Israel and to those who go to be judged before them (Laws of Sanhedrin 26:7): \"Whoever is judged by the laws of gentiles and in their courts, even if their laws were like the laws of Israel, is wicked, and it is as though he blasphemed and raised a hand against the Torah of Moshe our teacher.\"</p>\n<p>Indeed, there are Torah scholars and jurists who, on the basis of various reasonings, doubted the prohibition and its severe implications, claiming that whatever the public decides has validity, and the public may decide to judge according to human reasoning and various legal systems that have been proven successful in modern states. However, it seems that after the experience accumulated over the years, one can see plainly the severity of the sin and the magnitude of its punishment: the court is today the institution that most harms the Jewish identity of the State of Israel, its security and its ability to settle the Land.</p>\n<p>It must be noted that even after we merit to establish the ideal legal system according to the Torah of Israel, we will have to set an interim period in which all contracts, agreements and criminal cases will be judged according to the current legal system, so as not to bring down the social and economic systems that were based on it. Therefore the transition to Torah law must be gradual, in a way that no person is harmed by the change.<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Judge Prof. R. Menaḥem Elon in his book 'Ha-Mishpat Ha-Ivri' vol. 3 pp. 1605-1607, and Judge Prof. R. Yaakov Bazak, in Teḥumin 2 pp. 523-527, and Rav Prof. Yitzḥak Brand, hold that the secular courts in Israel do not have the law of 'gentile courts' before which it is forbidden to be judged. By contrast, the position accepted by the rabbis is that their law is like the law of 'gentile courts', and we will mention the famous among them. So wrote Rav Yitzḥak Herzog, the Chief Rabbi of Israel at the time of the establishment of the State: \"Now, when the people of Israel dwells in its Land and, to our heart's sorrow, judges according to foreign laws, the matter is a thousand times more severe than an individual or a community in Israel who go to be judged in gentile courts… and who can foresee the results of this shameful and disgraceful situation\" (Ha-Torah Ve-ha-Medina vol. 7; 'Teḥuka Le-Yisrael Al Pi Ha-Torah' vol. 1 pp. 148, 152, where he reacted sharply to the position of Rav Goren, who in his youth inclined to permit recourse to a secular court). And so held Rav Tzvi Pesaḥ Frank; Tzitz Eliezer 12:82; 'Ḥazon Ish' (Sanhedrin 15:4); Rav Mordechai Eliyahu (Teḥumin 3, p. 244); and Rav Ovadia Yosef (Yeḥaveh Da'at 4:65). In practice the secular legal establishment, and especially the highest court, is the state institution that most harms the Jewish identity of the State and its security. As an example we will mention some of the decisions of the legal system.<br><b>Harm to the Jewish identity of the State</b>: a) It harmed the status of the Hebrew language as the language of the State, almost equating the Arabic language with the Hebrew language. b) It prevented the disqualification of anti-Zionist parties and candidates from running for the Knesset, contrary to the position of the Elections Committee, which relied on the Basic Law: The Knesset, forbidding the candidacy of a party that denies the Jewish identity of the State. c) The court thwarted government decisions and laws legislated by the Knesset intended to expel African infiltrators from the country. d) The Attorney General prevented the State from opposing the reunification of Arab families on demographic grounds in order to preserve the Jewish majority, permitting opposition only on security grounds, and even this the High Court approved by a fragile majority. (These are a few examples.)<br><b>Harm in matters of halakha and the rabbinate:</b> a) In a series of decisions the court harmed the values of Shabbat, permitting more and more public desecrations of Shabbat (cinemas, shopping centres outside cities, \"convenience\" stores in the heart of Tel Aviv, etc.). b) In a series of decisions the High Court intervened in the discretion of city rabbis concerning the granting of kashrut. c) It obligated the batei din to rule according to secular law in monetary matters. d) It forbade the state batei din to serve as arbitrators under the Arbitration Law. e) The Knesset legislated the 'Foundations of Law Act', according to which, in any legal question that Israeli law has not decided, the court must turn to the values of the heritage of Israel; but in practice the Supreme Court emptied this law of content. f) The institution of the Attorney General forced the participation of women singers and actresses in official and semi-official ceremonies, without taking into account the position of halakha and of the religious and Ḥaredi public.<br><b>Harm to the value of settling the Land:</b> For a hundred and fifty years a national struggle has been taking place between the Jews and the Arabs over the Land of Israel. To redeem the Land and settle it, the Jewish National Fund was founded and afterwards the State of Israel. But the Supreme Court, in a gradual process, harms the ability of the State of Israel to realise its purpose: a) It forbade the government to allocate state lands for settlement intended for Jews only. b) It forbade giving incentives to Jewish settlements in the Galilee and the Negev (Judaising the Galilee and the Negev), and thereby annulled the ideal that accompanied the Zionist movement from its beginning. c) Even regarding the lands of the Jewish National Fund, which were bought with Jewish money only, following the deliberations of the Supreme Court, the Attorney General instructed not to prefer Jewish settlement any more. d) Following petitions of left-wing organisations, the court hurried to intervene and demand the evacuation of Jewish neighbourhoods and outposts in Judea and Samaria, while breaking the rules of procedure, which require legal proceedings to clarify ownership of the land before the magistrates' and district courts. e) The court forbade the State to spray from the air with herbicides plots of Bedouin in the Negev that had been sown illegally, although it was proven that this is an effective method for halting their takeover of state lands. f) The court forbade Prime Minister Netanyahu, at the end of his first term, to close Orient House, claiming that his government was a 'transition government', and by contrast rejected an identical petition against the Taba talks at the end of Barak's term. (These are examples only.)<br><b>Harm to the security of Israel:</b> a) The court forbade the shaking of Shin Bet detainees even in situations of a \"ticking bomb\". b) It annulled the \"neighbour procedure\", a method that saved the lives of many soldiers. c) It placed severe restrictions that tie the hands of the IDF regarding striking terrorists who are about to carry out an attack. d) It struck down a law legislated in the Knesset that permitted detaining \"hard-core\" terrorists for two weeks without seeing a judge, despite the security need for this in order to extract information from them. e) Contrary to the position of the security establishment, in many places it ordered the dismantling and moving of sections of the separation fence or of the settlements' fences, and likewise ordered opening roads and removing roadblocks, with clear knowledge that this might cause a security risk. f) In a series of decisions, the court and the legal establishment forced the IDF to recruit girls into combat units, contrary to the position of the professional committees.<br>At times the very fact that the High Court begins to deliberate on certain petitions causes the governing system to retreat from its plan. For example: the High Court held deliberations on left-wing petitions against reducing the supply of fuel, electricity and various goods to Gaza, and following this the Attorney General, Mr Mazuz, instructed the government to back down from its plan. This brief survey suffices to determine that the legal system is today the most alienated establishment, and the one that most harms the values of Judaism and Zionism in Israel.<br>It must be noted that the legal establishment also has many positive sides: in the area of derekh eretz, which preceded the Torah, it conducts itself reasonably, and by its very existence it helps preserve law and order and the proper existence of the social and economic system. And even if it did not conduct itself at a reasonable level of fairness, the words of the Sages would still apply to its very existence: \"Pray for the welfare of the kingdom, for were it not for fear of it, people would swallow one another alive\" (Avot 3:2)."}]},{"slug":"06-06-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-08/","title_he":"ח – תיקון המשפט","title_en":"Repairing the Law","body_en":"<p>This situation, in which the courts in the State of Israel judge according to a foreign system, constitutes a terrible desecration of the Name. Therefore a great obligation rests on those who learn Torah to prepare a legal infrastructure that will be able to replace the legal system, and as long as they do not prepare it, those who learn Torah too are full partners in the responsibility for the desecration of the Name. And since there is not yet a legal infrastructure according to the Torah that can replace the legal system, one can judge the Israeli jurists favourably, that it was not out of malice that they turned their backs on Torah law. Rather, since they thought they would not succeed in sustaining a modern society and economy according to the laws of the Torah, they turned to the law that was already prevalent in the Land, which began with the Turkish laws and is principally according to English law; and from there onward they continued to build the legal system according to the enormous experience acquired in the legal systems of the democratic states, through the work of hundreds of thousands of jurists who, by way of clarification, trial, error and correction, created a sophisticated system suited to modern society. According to this, the words of the Rambam should not be applied to one who supports the secular legal system (Laws of Sanhedrin 26:7): \"Whoever is judged by the laws of gentiles and in their courts… is wicked, and it is as though he blasphemed and raised a hand against the Torah of Moshe our teacher.\"</p>\n<p>And from here to the question of appointing religious judges. Some rabbis hold that it is forbidden for one who keeps Torah and mitzvot to be appointed as a judge in a secular court in the State of Israel. By contrast, some rabbis hold that since the public, through its representatives in the Knesset, accepted upon itself the secular legal system, it is permitted to sustain it and to be appointed as a judge, for this judge is not to blame for setting the foreign legal system. And it seems to my humble opinion that everything depends on the position of the judge. If he is pained that people do not conduct themselves according to Torah law, and tries as far as he can to repair it, then there is value in his being appointed as a judge. He of course cannot break his commitment to rule according to the agreed law, but in cases where the law is open to interpretation, he is commanded to stretch the interpretation in the direction of Torah law. But if he integrates into the system and accepts its moral yoke wholeheartedly, even if from time to time he adorns his words with verses and sayings of the Sages, he is a partner in the desecration of the Name (so it seems from the Rishon Le-Tziyon Rav Mordechai Eliyahu zt\"l, Teḥumin 3 p. 244).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"One must admit that those who learn Torah too are partners in the responsibility for the situation. If we merit and the people awakens tomorrow to repentance, and the members of the Knesset and the government decide to change the legal system and ask the rabbis to set up a legal system according to halakha, the rabbis will have, for lack of choice, to ask the secular legal system to continue managing legal affairs. For to our heart's sorrow, we have not yet prepared the learning infrastructure for establishing a legal system according to the Torah. We have not yet trained judges who will be able to fill the place of today's judges; we do not yet have a consolidated position regarding rules of procedure, evidence, criminal prosecution, contract law, labour law, public law, taxes, limited companies and the like. In addition, in many topics there is perplexity, for the Rishonim disagreed about them, and because of the exile the Sages of Israel did not have the power to decide the halakha, and each of the litigants can claim \"kim li\" according to the view of so-and-so, that is, in my opinion the halakha follows the view of the rabbi who justifies my position. And since the halakha was not decided, one cannot rule who is right. In order for us to be able to establish a legal system according to the Torah, we will first need to rule clear halakha in all the fundamental topics.<br>Therefore one can judge favourably the Israeli jurists who relied on the experience accumulated in modern states and on its basis established a legal system in the State of Israel. Indeed, in practice, since the secular legal system draws its values from Western society with its various ills, it is impervious to the values of the Torah, the people and the Land, and systematically robs the Jewish people of its values, its rights, its Land and its national destiny, as brought in the previous note."}]},{"slug":"06-06-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-09/","title_he":"ט – מצוות מינוי שופטים","title_en":"The Mitzva of Appointing Judges","body_en":"<p>It is a Torah mitzva to appoint in every single city judges who will judge the people with righteous judgement, and officers who will enforce its fulfilment, as it is said (Devarim 16:18): \"Judges and officers you shall appoint for yourself in all your gates, which the Lord your God gives you, according to your tribes, and they shall judge the people with righteous judgement.\" And the intention is to appoint a small Sanhedrin of twenty-three judges in every city, which can judge all the laws of the Torah, capital cases and monetary cases. Capital cases are judged before twenty-three judges, and monetary cases before three judges (Sanhedrin 2a). But if there were not in the city two sages, one who knows how to rule in the whole Torah and another sage who knows how to listen, to ask and to answer in the whole Torah, and in addition to them twenty-one decent and wise men fit to judge, one does not seat a Sanhedrin there. But one seats there at least a beit din of three who will judge monetary cases (Rambam, Laws of Sanhedrin 1:4-5).</p>\n<p>But as we have learned, the authority of the Sages to judge all the laws of the Torah depends on the ordination by which they are ordained one from the mouth of another back to Moshe our teacher; but after ordination was annulled, about three hundred years after the destruction of the Second Temple, the Sages of Israel have no independent authority to judge Torah laws, but they judge Torah laws as agents of the Sages who were ordained in the past. This agency was intended solely for the areas necessary for the existence of Jewish life, such as monetary law and accepting converts. But in areas where there is no necessity to judge, Sages who are not ordained have no authority to judge. Therefore the batei din today have no authority to judge capital cases and fines. And only as a temporary measure are they permitted to fine and punish (Gittin 88b; Bava Kamma 84b; SA ḤM 1:1; 2:1).</p>\n<p>Since today the judges have no independent authority to judge all the laws of the Torah, the Torah mitzva to establish a Sanhedrin in every single city has lapsed (Ramban, Devarim 16:18; Tur ḤM 1). But it is a mitzva to establish batei din for monetary cases in every single city, in order to uphold justice in Israel.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-10/","title_he":"י – שלא להשהות את הדין","title_en":"Not Delaying Judgement","body_en":"<p>There is great importance in ending every dispute as quickly as possible. As the Sages said (Sanhedrin 7a), disputes between a person and his fellow are like a crack in a dam: at first it can easily be sealed, but if it is not sealed immediately, the crack will widen and much water will begin to rush through it, and then it will no longer be possible to seal the crack, because the whole dam will collapse in the flood of water. So it is with a quarrel: if it is resolved at its beginning, both sides are reconciled and become friends again; but if it is not resolved at its beginning, the hatred becomes fixed in the hearts and it is very hard to remove it. Therefore the Sages warned not to delay judgement ('innui ha-din'), that is, not to hold up the giving of the verdict (see Avot 5:8).</p>\n<p>And likewise the Torah commanded to establish judges in every single city, as it is said (Devarim 16:18): \"Judges and officers you shall appoint for yourself in all your gates, which the Lord your God gives you, according to your tribes.\" And the judges must sit at the gate of the city, in the place where all the people pass, so that they will be available to all, and so every dispute will be able to be clarified before them immediately. And the verdict too would be carried out quickly, and if one of the litigants refused to fulfil the judgement, the officers who stood at the disposal of the beit din would immediately enforce the judgement.</p>\n<p>At first there were no special days on which the beit din would sit in judgement; rather on all weekdays they would sit from the morning until midday (Rambam, Laws of Sanhedrin 3:1). In the days of the establishment of the Second Temple, Ezra enacted that they sit on every Monday and Thursday, because in most places there is no need to judge every day. And he set Monday and Thursday because then the villagers would come to the cities, and so the villagers too could clarify their disputes before the judges.</p>\n<p>To understand the importance of clarifying judgement quickly, one can give a negative example from the existing reality in the civil courts in Israel. In addition to the fact that according to halakha they are considered gentile courts (as explained in halakha 7), judgement there is greatly delayed. Much time passes from the time of the claim until the beginning of the hearing, and afterwards much time passes until the verdict arrives. During the time of the delay of judgement the disputes become more severe, and when the verdict finally arrives, the hour is already late and it does not have the power to restore peace. In addition, delaying judgement gives a great advantage to the rich over the poor, because the rich man knows that whenever he has a dispute with a poor businessman, he will bring the dispute to the courts, and by the time the verdict arrives, the poor businessman will collapse from a shortage of cash. Therefore many poor people, who have no economic ability to drag out a trial over years, agree to compromise and receive less than what is due to them.</p>\n<p>Also, when a criminal charge is filed against a person, his name is stained for a long period, and even if in the end he is acquitted, the long time it takes until the end of his trial harms him greatly and may leave deep scars in his heart that may not heal until his last day.</p>\n<p>To our sorrow, even the batei din that judge according to halakha are not properly available. The main reason for this is that they are not properly budgeted from the public purse. However, even now, when the Torah batei din are not recognised by law as courts in every respect, one should strive that next to every yeshiva and kollel panels of three judges for monetary matters be established, who will be available every day to hold hearings. These judges must be expert in halakha, in economics and in law. When people come before them for judgement, they will judge; and when people do not come, they will occupy themselves with Torah within the framework of the yeshiva or kollel. In this way we will merit to increase justice and peace and to bring the redemption near, and the vision of Yeshayahu will be fulfilled in us (1:26-27): \"And I will restore your judges as at the first, and your counsellors as at the beginning; afterwards you shall be called the city of righteousness, the faithful city. Zion shall be redeemed with judgement, and those who return to her with righteousness.\"</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-11/","title_he":"יא – נכון ליישב סכסוכי עבודה בבית דין","title_en":"It Is Right to Settle Labour Disputes in a Beit Din","body_en":"<p>Question: Is a workers' strike the fitting way to settle labour disputes according to halakha?</p>\n<p>Answer: First one must preface that in practice, if the custom of the country is that workers strike in order to improve their wages, they are permitted to strike. According to most poskim, this permission is conditional on receiving permission for it from a beit din. However, from the outset this is not the right way; rather it would be fitting to resolve all labour disputes by litigation before an agreed beit din. Only in this way will the problem indeed be resolved completely. First, no economic damage will be caused by shutting down factories and losing work days, and no social tension will be caused. And more importantly, the verdict will be balanced and just, and not like the situation today, in which workers who have great power of extortion succeed in obtaining very high salaries, whereas other workers, whose work is not inferior in importance and who invest no less effort, receive almost a quarter of their wages, because their strike does not harm those who have influence over the salaries in the country.</p>\n<p>And so it was reported that this was the view of our master Rav Kook zt\"l: that every labour dispute must come before a beit din, and only when the employer does not agree to hold arbitration is it permitted to stop work. And so too wrote Rav Uziel zt\"l, that one should establish a beit din in which there will be Torah scholars and experts in economics and society, and they will judge every labour dispute (Teḥumin 5, p. 295; Mishpetei Uziel ḤM 42:6-7). In this way the economy will be able to flourish without stoppages and coercive pressures, and the salaries of all the workers too will be higher.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-12/","title_he":"יב – האם מותר לפועלים לשבות","title_en":"Whether Workers May Strike","body_en":"<p>A worker who committed himself to work for a certain period is forbidden to break his commitment, and if he broke his commitment, the employer may have a grievance against him, but cannot sue him for compensation. And if the worker's strike causes monetary damage to the employer, the employer may sue the worker for the harm to his business, and he must compensate him for the damages caused through breaking his commitment to work. And one who is not satisfied with his wages may stop his work at the end of the period set in the contract, but he should not stop in the middle.</p>\n<p>This is the halakha in principle; but the people of a town, or the members of a certain trade, have the right to set regulations and customs for themselves, and this includes establishing the right to strike, even within the period the worker committed himself to work. But all this is on condition that these regulations are enacted with the consent of a 'ḥever ir', that is, with the consent of a beit din or of a person great in Torah who is involved in public affairs, so that that beit din or Torah scholar will examine whether there is injustice in their regulations.</p>\n<p>The question is whether today, when the rabbis and the batei din do not supervise public affairs, the people of a town or the members of a trade may enact regulations for themselves not according to the original halakha. The view of several poskim is that in a place where there are no Torah scholars involved in and influencing all public affairs, workers' organisations are permitted to organise strikes as they see fit (Igrot Moshe ḤM 1:58-59). By contrast, many poskim hold that workers are forbidden to strike without the consent of a beit din (Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach, Teḥumin 5, 289; Tzitz Eliezer 2:23; Aseh Lekha Rav 2:64). In their view, a worker who wishes to strike must ask a qualified rabbi to decide for him in each and every case on its merits whether he is permitted to strike or forbidden.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-13/","title_he":"יג – שביתת מורים ורופאים","title_en":"Strikes by Teachers and Doctors","body_en":"<p>All that we have learned about the right to strike is true regarding ordinary work in which there is no mitzva; but teachers who teach Torah are forbidden to strike. For whoever can teach Torah is commanded to teach even for free, and the wages given to teachers are so that they will not need to seek another livelihood and will be free to teach. But if the teacher does not go to seek another livelihood, he must continue to teach the children. And likewise regarding doctors and nurses: since there is a mitzva to heal for free, even if their salary does not fit their efforts, they are forbidden to strike. Of course they are permitted to seek other work, but as long as they have not gone to work elsewhere, since they are available, they are obligated to continue to heal for free. Labour disputes in these professions of mitzva must be resolved through arbitration and not through strikes.</p>\n<p>Only in rare cases, when the employer is not willing to come to judgement or arbitration, and it seems that if they do not strike harm will be caused to the education system or to public medicine, one may consult a beit din, and after receiving permission, it is permitted to strike. For sometimes one must set aside Torah or kindness so that it will be possible to sustain them in the long term.</p>\n<p>Therefore a teacher who teaches Torah is forbidden to take part in a strike that has not received explicit approval from an important beit din or from the local rabbinate. And even if the teachers' organisation threatens him that if he ever needs help in the future they will not help him, the teacher is forbidden to take part in the strike. And just as, if they threatened him, \"If you do not eat non-kosher meat we will harm your rights\", he would not eat non-kosher meat, so if they threaten him that if he does not strike they will not represent him in the future, he should not strike. And in this merit he will merit to be a true educator whose life's aim is spiritual values, and in this he will serve as an example to his students, who will follow in his footsteps for the glory of the Torah, the people and the Land (see Igrot Moshe ḤM 1:59; Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach, Teḥumin 5, 289; Minḥat Tzvi vol. 2, 10).</p>\n<p>Women teachers too, who teach girls Torah, are forbidden to strike. And one should not say that women are not obligated in the mitzva of Torah study, and consequently there is no annulment of a mitzva in their strike and they are permitted to strike. For in truth women too are commanded to learn all the mitzvot that pertain to them and all the foundations of faith. It follows that the women teachers who teach the girls halakha and faith also fulfil a mitzva. And girls who feel a need to learn more, it is a sign that their learning is important to them in order to deepen faith and their belonging to the Torah, and this learning too becomes a mitzva and it is forbidden to annul it (as explained in Peninei Halakha, Women's Prayer 7:2).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-14/","title_he":"יד – האם מותר למורים ללימודי חול לשבות","title_en":"Whether Teachers of Secular Subjects May Strike","body_en":"<p>Some hold that only holy studies may not be stopped by a strike, but secular studies, since there is no mitzva to learn them, teachers may stop like any other work. And we have already learned that according to most poskim one must receive permission for this from an important rabbi.</p>\n<p>However, in my humble opinion it seems that in every institution in which holy studies are the main thing and secular studies are added to them, the secular studies too may not be stopped by a strike. For when secular studies are done alongside holy studies, they too are considered a branch of mitzva. For the Sages said (Shabbat 75a): \"Whoever knows how to calculate the seasons and the constellations and does not calculate, about him the verse says (Yeshayahu 5:12): 'But they regard not the work of the Lord, neither have they considered the work of His hands.'\" And the Rambam, the Maharal and many of the great ones of Israel explain that the intention is not that only the wisdom of astronomy is fitting to learn, but that whoever can understand with wisdom the orders of created nature and does not understand ignores the work of God, for the Holy One, blessed be He, is revealed to us through creation and science. It is further explained there in the Gemara that through Israel being expert in the external wisdoms, a sanctification of God's Name is made before the eyes of the nations. And the Gra said that whoever lacks knowledge in the external wisdoms lacks correspondingly a hundred measures in Torah (Peninei Halakha, Likkutim vol. 1, 1:14).</p>\n<p>It follows that even secular studies, when they are conducted within a programme whose main part is holy, are considered as assisting and belonging to education for faith and Torah, and it is forbidden to annul them by a strike. In other words, when the aim of the whole system of learning is for the sake of Heaven, to educate the sons and daughters to live complete lives of faith in this world, then the whole system of learning is considered one piece, and it is forbidden to stop it by a strike, for all of it is a mitzva.</p>\n<p>But in a place where holy studies are not emphasised, that is, they do not receive the central place, either in terms of hours or in terms of importance, the secular studies are considered as standing by themselves, and consequently the law of teachers of secular studies is like the law of all workers, who are permitted to strike according to the custom of the country.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-15/","title_he":"טו – מלכות דוד והאם נהגו בית חשמונאי כדין","title_en":"The Kingdom of David, and Whether the Hasmoneans Acted Lawfully","body_en":"<p>Towards the end of the chapter we will mention a halakha concerning the kingdom. It is said (Bereshit 49:10): \"The sceptre shall not depart from Yehuda, nor the lawgiver from between his feet\", and the Sages explained (Sanhedrin 5a): \"'The sceptre shall not depart from Yehuda': these are the Exilarchs in Babylonia, who rule Israel with a rod. 'Nor the lawgiver from between his feet': these are the descendants of Hillel, who teach Torah in public.\" The Ramban noted precisely from the expression \"shall not depart\" that only after David was appointed king may the heads of the people no longer be appointed from other tribes; but before that it was possible to appoint a king from other tribes. Therefore Shaul was appointed from the tribe of Binyamin. The Ramban further explained that since at that time Israel requested the king in an improper way, for Shmuel the prophet led them according to God in a complete way, God commanded Shmuel to appoint Shaul, who was from the tribe of Binyamin.</p>\n<p>The Ramban added: \"And in my opinion, the kings who reigned over Israel from the other tribes after David were transgressing the will of their father and transferring an inheritance.\" Only for the needs of the hour may a king be appointed from another tribe, until a king from the tribe of Yehuda is appointed; and therefore the Sages said that if a king is appointed from another tribe, he is not anointed, in order to emphasise that his kingship is temporary. And for this the Hasmoneans, who reigned in the days of the Second Temple, were punished. And although they were supremely pious, and were it not for them the Torah and the mitzvot would have been forgotten from Israel, they were punished with a great punishment: the four sons of the elder Hasmonean, the pious ones who reigned one after another, with all their heroism and success, fell by the sword into the hands of their enemies, until in the end Herod cut off all their seed (Bava Batra 3b). For they should have striven for the appointment of a king from the tribe of Yehuda. Not only this, but there was a further sin in their hands: they were kohanim, and the kohanim were commanded to guard their priesthood, whereas they served at the same time in the role of king and of High Priest. It is reasonable that the occupation with the affairs of kingship disturbed the holy service, and who knows whether this sin did not cause that the Shekhina did not return to rest in the Second Temple as it rested in the First Temple. And likewise from the other side, it is reasonable that the occupation with the priesthood harmed the full devotion to the affairs of the kingdom.</p>\n<p>Indeed, according to the Rashba (Responsa 4:187), there is no command not to appoint a king from another tribe, but rather a promise that the kingship of the tribe of Yehuda will be permanent. And the Ran in his Derashot (derush 7) holds that there is no complaint against the Hasmonean kings, for Yaakov's testament was that only when Israel has an independent kingdom will it be given into the hands of the tribe of Yehuda; but the Hasmoneans reigned under the patronage of the Greek kingdoms and afterwards under the patronage of Rome, and therefore there is no prohibition in their hands. Nevertheless, many accepted the view of the Ramban, that the Hasmoneans should have made more effort to appoint a king from the tribe of Yehuda and to separate the kingship from the priesthood. As the people of the Land of Israel in the period of the Tanna'im and Amora'im made efforts to appoint Nesi'im from the tribe of Yehuda, and although they did not descend through sons, in any case they descended through daughters (see further Tzitz Eliezer 19:26).</p>\n<p>It is worth mentioning that the idea of the separation of powers accepted in modern states has its foundation in the Torah, where the division in the Torah is into four authorities: a) kingship; b) judges, where all the fundamental decisions were set by the Great Beit Din in Jerusalem, and the individual cases were judged in the batei din in every single place; c) priesthood, whose service is centred in the Temple, and whose branches spread throughout all the borders of Israel, in all the cities of the kohanim; d) prophecy, which would set out a vision, warn and criticise, and also deal with temporary rulings. In this way the kingship is constitutional, that is, the law of the Torah is above the king, and therefore the king is commanded to carry the Torah with him everywhere (Devarim 17:19).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-06-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-06-16/","title_he":"טז – בחירת אשה לחברת כנסת ולשרה","title_en":"Electing a Woman as a Member of Knesset or a Minister","body_en":"<p>It is said in the Torah (Devarim 17:15): \"You shall surely set a king over yourself\", and the Sages expounded (Sifrei there): \"A king and not a queen.\" And the Rambam (Kings 1:5) extended this halakha to all positions of authority in Israel, writing: \"One does not set a woman in kingship, as it is said 'a king over yourself' and not a queen; and likewise in all appointments in Israel only a man is appointed.\" On this basis some say that one should not appoint a woman as a member of Knesset (Rav Tikoczinski).</p>\n<p>On the other hand, several of the Rishonim wrote that the instruction of the Torah not to appoint a woman concerns specifically kingship; but regarding other leadership, if the public accepts upon itself a woman for leadership or judgement, there is no flaw in this; and so we find that the public accepted upon itself the leadership of Devora the prophetess, who judged Israel (Rashba and Ran, Shevuot 30a). And so wrote Rav Uziel (Piskei Uziel 44) and Rav Herzog, and in their view the words of the Rambam forbidding the appointment of a woman to any position of authority were not accepted as halakha.</p>\n<p>And some say that public representatives elected within a democratic framework are not considered holders of authority, and according to this, even according to the Rambam it is permitted today to elect a woman as a member of Knesset, a minister and a prime minister (Rav Yisraeli, Amud Ha-Yemini 12:5).</p>\n<p>Thus although there are those who disagree, in any case according to most poskim, if the public wishes it, it is permitted to elect a woman member of Knesset or minister.</p>\n<p>Indeed, there is another topic here, which is the question of modesty. In a place where it is not accepted that a woman occupy herself with public affairs and leadership, consequently occupation with this is not modest. However, there is no absolute prohibition in this, and therefore if there is a great need for it, it is fitting that a suitable woman fill public roles, even when it is not accepted. And when the matter becomes accepted, as in our days in the Knesset and the government, there is no prohibition on account of modesty.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"The conduct of modesty is the reason why in the year 5680 (1920) our master Rav Kook zt\"l opposed granting the right to vote to women. For at that time in most countries the right to vote had not been granted to women. Only two years before that had the right to vote been granted to women in England, and from that period onward more and more countries began to grant women the right to vote: in the USA the right was granted that same year, in France more than twenty years later, in 1944, and in Switzerland in 1971 women were granted the right to vote. And since the matter was not yet widespread in the world, the great rabbis regarded it as something contrary to the customs of modesty, just as there were periods when driving a car was an immodest act for women.<br>But our master Rav Kook did not hold that it was a halakhic prohibition, and therefore he did not agree to sign, together with the rabbis of the Eda Ḥaredit, Rav Diskin and Rav Sonnenfeld, a document determining that it is forbidden; rather he wrote a different wording, according to which the participation of women is \"against the law of Moshe and of Jewish practice, and against the spirit of the nation in general\", that is, contrary to the conduct of modesty prevalent in Israel. The great majority of the rabbis signed this wording. After his view was not accepted, and the British government determined that the right to vote would be granted to women, our master Rav Kook did not go out to fight against the participation of women in the elections or against those elected, and it seems that he did not even oppose women voting in practice.<br>It should be noted that even then there were rabbis who held that occupying oneself with this question was a mistake. So held, for example, one of the friends and admirers of our master Rav Kook, the Gaon Rav Avraham Shapira of Kovno, author of 'Devar Avraham', who wrote to Rav Tzvi Yehuda zt\"l that he was very surprised at his father our master the Rav for entering this question, for what prohibition is there in it? And indeed even the greatest sage may err; nevertheless, it is better to listen to the counsel of the sages, for one who listens less to the sages errs more.<br>And although with the wisdom of hindsight it seems that it would have been better for our master the Rav zt\"l not to enter this question, on second thought it may be that his position was justified for that time. For the Jewish public in the Land was then very divided between Ḥaredim and secular, and our master the Rav tried with all his strength to unite the ranks, and the participation of women in the elections greatly distanced the Ḥaredi public from participation in the public institutions. Had our master the Rav succeeded in creating cooperation between the Ḥaredi public and the Zionist-secular public, the people would have been built in its Land with incomparably great strength. The large Ḥaredi public in the Land and in Europe would have felt a belonging to the building of the Land, and the secular public would have been more connected to its heritage, and very many of the ills that exist today in the State of Israel would have been resolved. The fact that we did not merit this does not mean that the attempt to create a framework of life suited to everyone together was mistaken."}]}]});
